          "title": "Depth Laws for the Precision Floor of Trained Neural Networks: Amplification, Residual Scaling, and a Quantization-Aware Training Paradox",
          "title_zh": "训练神经网络精度下限的深度定律：放大效应、残差缩放与量化感知训练的悖论",
          "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32060v1",
          "source": "arXiv",
          "section": "arxiv_other",
          "organization": "",
          "language": "en",
          "arxiv_id": "2609.32060",
          "summary": "The paper derives a first-order theory in which the precision floor at which accuracy falls halfway to chance is set by a predictive amplification factor that grows linearly with depth, and tests it in MLPs, CNNs, Vision Transformers, and nine pretrained language models under post-training quantization and quantization- or noise-aware training. The predicted depth-exponent equality holds in twelve of thirteen architectures and in GPT-2 from 12 to 48 layers, and the authors report a QAT paradox in which noise-aware training's benefit decays with depth, steepening the depth law.",
